window.docContents[54].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Mean-values___-derivative-estimates___-and-rigidity":{"contents":"Sphere and ball averages are defined as normalized integrals, and the coarea\nformula connects them. Harmonicity implies the sphere mean-value property,\nwhich in turn yields the ball mean-value formula. In one dimension the\nparallel result is proved directly: a  function with zero second\nderivative is affine on each relevant interval and obeys the interval\nmean-value identity.\n\nThe first-derivative estimates are proved from the ball Poisson formula and\nmean values. On an open set in positive dimension, classical harmonicity\nimplies interior smoothness and harmonicity of every fixed iterated\nderivative evaluation. The arbitrary-order estimates therefore need only\nharmonicity of  and a bound on  itself. For , they give\n when the closed ball of radius \nlies in the domain and  there. Liouville's theorem holds in its\nbounded form: a globally bounded harmonic function on  is\nconstant.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Fundamental solutions and classical harmonic functions","header":"5.7.1.1.2. Mean values, derivative estimates, and rigidity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Fundamental-solutions-and-classical-harmonic-functions--Mean-values___-derivative-estimates___-and-rigidity"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Propagation, shocks, and weak selection","header":"7.1.1. Propagation and characteristics","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Propagation___-shocks___-and-weak-selection--Propagation-and-characteristics"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Time-dependent-potential-flow":{"contents":"The preceding calculation also works when an irrotational flow changes with\ntime. Suppose we can write the velocity and force as spatial gradients,\n and . Mixed derivatives commute, so the time\nderivative of velocity is ; the convective acceleration is\n. The entire momentum equation is now one spatial\ngradient:\n\n\n\nWe can make it vanish by choosing the pressure. Incompressibility leaves a\nseparate condition on the potential: . This is the\npotential-function reduction of Evans, §4.4.2.\n\nIn any spatial dimension, let  be  at a spacetime point and let\n be differentiable there. DefineThen  is irrotational and satisfies the pointwise momentum equationMoreover,  exactly when . On an open\nregion with  and , the constructed velocity and pressure\nare , so spatial harmonicity gives a classical Euler flow throughout\nthat region.\n\nThe antisymmetric velocity derivative is zero because its two terms are\nmixed second derivatives of . The irrotational convective-acceleration\nidentity therefore turns  into the kinetic-energy\ngradient. Commute time and space derivatives, then combine the four\ngradients in the momentum residual. Substituting the displayed pressure\nmakes their scalar potential identically zero. Taking the divergence of\n gives the spatial Laplacian directly.\n\nLet  and suppose\n for . Given Euler initial-value data with force\n and initial velocity , the velocity\n and pressure  form a global\nsmooth solution. In particular,If spatial harmonicity is supplied at time zero as well, the data constructor\nderives distributional incompressibility of  from the classical\ndivergence identity. The harmonic potential is part of the input.\n\nDifferentiating a smooth potential preserves smoothness of the velocity;\nthe time derivative and squared norm in the pressure do the same for\npressure. The pointwise reduction supplies momentum balance and\nincompressibility at every positive time. Restriction of the smooth\nvelocity to the initial slice gives the prescribed trace. At time zero,\nthe classical-to-distributional divergence theorem turns the vanishing\nLaplacian into the initial data's weak incompressibility condition.\n\nThe time derivative in the pressure is visible in a simple planar example.\nTake  and zero force. Then\n\n\n\nThe first pressure term balances ; the second balances convective\nacceleration. The compiled example PDE.Examples.Fluid.PotentialFlow checks\nthe derivatives and harmonicity with the CAS, constructs the smooth solution,\nand checks its initial trace. Its velocity grows linearly in space, so the\nflow has infinite energy on the whole plane.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Euler, Symmetry, and Boussinesq Flow\u0009Incompressible Euler Flow","header":"8.4.1.6. Time-dependent potential flow","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Euler___-Symmetry___-and-Boussinesq-Flow--Incompressible-Euler-Flow--Time-dependent-potential-flow"}});